<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Lullaby · 1868 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Wiegenlied (Brahms's Lullaby)</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="de">Wiegenlied</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A lullaby Johannes Brahms wrote in 1868 for a friend's new baby boy; it is one of the best-known cradle songs.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional (Des Knaben Wunderhorn); second verse adapted from Georg Scherer</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Johannes Brahms</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>German-speaking Europe</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>published November 1868; first public performance 22 December 1869</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Lullaby</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words from the folk-poem collection Des Knaben Wunderhorn and from an 1849 poem by Georg Scherer; music by Johannes Brahms (published 1868))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Wiegenlied&quot; (&quot;Lullaby&quot; or &quot;Cradle Song&quot;), Op. 49, No. 4, is a song for voice and piano by Johannes Brahms and one of his most famous pieces. He dedicated it to his friend Bertha Faber on the birth of her second son. Brahms had been in love with her when she was young, and he built the melody so that a song she used to sing to him runs beneath it as a hidden counter-melody. The music also draws in part on &quot;S'Is Anderscht&quot;, a duet by Alexander Baumann published in the 1840s.</p><p>The words come from Des Knaben Wunderhorn, a collection of German folk poems. In its first publication the song had a single verse; Brahms later adapted a second verse from an 1849 poem by Georg Scherer. Simrock published Brahms's Op. 49 in November 1868, and the lullaby was first performed in public on 22 December 1869 in Vienna, by the singer Luise Dustmann with Clara Schumann at the piano.</p><p>The song has been called deceptively simple. In 1877 Brahms used its tune as the basis of the second theme of the first movement of his Second Symphony. It became one of his most popular songs and has been arranged and recorded many times: Percy Grainger set it for solo piano in 1922, a 1936 film about Brahms, with Albert Florath as the composer, took its title from the song's opening words, and the poet Wendy Cope wrote a poem about it. In 2005 the scholar Karen Bottge analysed it as an expression of the maternal voice.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A cradle song sung over a child at bedtime. The mood is quiet and tender.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">German</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">German</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Guten Abend, gut’ Nacht,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Good evening, good night,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">mit Rosen bedacht,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Covered over with roses,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">mit Näglein besteckt,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Stuck all over with carnations,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">schlupf unter die Deck’:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Slip under the blanket:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Morgen früh, wenn Gott will,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Tomorrow morning, if God wills,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">wirst du wieder geweckt.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You will be woken again.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Guten Abend, gut’ Nacht,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Good evening, good night,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">von Englein bewacht,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Watched over by little angels,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">die zeigen im Traum</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Who show you in your dream</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">dir Christkindleins Baum.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The Christ child's tree.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Schlaf nun selig und süß,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Sleep now blissful and sweet,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">schau im Traum ’s Paradies.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>See paradise in your dream.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Des Knaben Wunderhorn and Georg Scherer, as Brahms set them (1868); as quoted in Wikipedia's article (German Wikipedia). Text from <a href="https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guten_Abend%2C_gut%E2%80%99_Nacht" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (2)</summary><ul><li>line 1.3: 'Näglein' (little nails) is an old word for carnations (also cloves).</li><li>line 2.4: 'Christkindleins Baum' is the Christmas tree, the tree of the Christ child.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
