<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Symphony No. 2, first movement (Johannes Brahms)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1877</span><span class="sg-chain-h">its tune is the basis of the second theme</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Free Settings of Favorite Melodies (Percy Grainger)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1922</span><span class="sg-chain-h">piano arrangement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Biographical film of Brahms, titled from the song's opening words</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1936</span><span class="sg-chain-h">title</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Brahms Cradle Song (Wendy Cope)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">poem that refers to the song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Among My Souvenirs (Joni James)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1958</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Brahms Lullaby, in Miracles: The Holiday Album (Kenny G)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1994</span><span class="sg-chain-h">instrumental recording</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Des Knaben Wunderhorn</span><span class="sg-chain-h">source of the first verse</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Poem by Georg Scherer</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1849</span><span class="sg-chain-h">source of the second verse, adapted</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">S'Is Anderscht (Alexander Baumann)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1840s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">duet the music partly draws on</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">A song Bertha Faber used to sing to Brahms</span><span class="sg-chain-h">hidden counter-melody</span></li></ul></section>
